      "traceability_score": 100,
      "evidence_status": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED",
      "knowledge_summary": "While global civil society does not deliver global democracy, it does contribute to more transparent, more deliberative and more ethical international decision-making which is ultimately preferable to a world of isolated sovereign states with no accountability outside their borders, or exclusive and secretive state-to-state diplomacy. A new examination of the International Criminal Court (ICC) from a political science and international relations perspective. It also delivers essential background to the place of the US in international relations and makes a major contribution to thinking about the ICC’s future. This book will be of great interest to students and scholars of international relations, international law, globalization and global governance. It also makes these issues accessible to non-lawyers and presents effective advocacy strategies for non-governmental organizations.",
